TzD (task #59): kfree / kmem_cache_free destroy the object in two stages
and drop its bounds entries (__tzd_wipe), the release rule libc2d's
free() follows (TK 2026-11-07).  Without it a freed object's entries
stay live and slab pages are reused elsewhere with stale records.
Caches with a ctor, SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU and SLAB_POISON are left alone,
as init_on_free does.  Whether this hook can become a weakness: task #68.
--- a/mm/slub.c
+++ b/mm/slub.c
@@ -2338,6 +2338,21 @@
 	if (kasan_slab_pre_free(s, x))
 		return false;
 
+#ifdef __TZD__
+	/*
+	 * TzD (task #59): every release destroys the memory in two stages
+	 * (zero, then meaningless bytes) and drops its bounds entries, as
+	 * libc2d's free() does.  Before the freepointer is written.  The
+	 * caches init_on_free leaves alone are left alone here too
+	 * (slab_want_init_on_free): a ctor's state must survive a free (the
+	 * ctor runs once per slab, shmem_inode_cache), SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU
+	 * objects stay readable, SLAB_POISON has its own pattern.  Task #68
+	 * looks at this hook as a whole.
+	 */
+	if (!s->ctor && !(s->flags & (SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU | SLAB_POISON)))
+		__tzd_wipe(x, s->object_size);
+#endif
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_SLUB_RCU_DEBUG
 	if (still_accessible) {
 		struct rcu_delayed_free *delayed_free;
